{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 0,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks to the Great Course Plus for supporting PBS Digital Studios."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4180,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This episode of space time is brought to you\nfrom the information flowing through an impossibly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7620,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "complex network of quantum entanglement, that\njust happens to mutually agree that you and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11800,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I exist inside it. Oh, and Schrodinger’s\ncat is in here too."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20880,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the quantum world things are routinely in multiple\nstates at once - what we call a “superposition”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26600,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of states.\" But in the classical world of large\nscales, things are either this or that. The"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33440,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "famous thought experiment is Schrodinger’s\ncat - in which a cat is in an opaque box with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38400,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a vial of deadly poison that’s released\non the radioactive decay of an atom."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44320,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum mechanics tells us that the atom’s wavefunction can be in a superposition of states - simultaneously"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decayed or not decayed. So is the cat’s\nwavefunction also in a superposition of both"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55960,
    "dDurationMs": 1880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dead and alive?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57920,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That seems absurd - but quantum mechanics\nappears to allow this. In fact the idea of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63280,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "superposition is fundamental to quantum mechanics.\nPick any two valid quantum states - the sum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70080,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of those states is also a valid state. In\nfact, at the quantum level, there isn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75600,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a clear way to define what states are the\nmost basic. Even very distinct-seeming properties"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81840,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can be expressed as superpositions - for example\na particle’s position can be expressed as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88400,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a superposition of momentum states. I’ll\ncome back to another example of this in a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93680,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "second. But this raises the question: for Schroedinger’s\ncat, if alive AND dead are just as valid as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101120,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "alive OR dead, why do we only see the later\nin the real world?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108080,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The idea of decoherence seems to provide a\npartial answer - we talked about it last time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113760,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- to observe a superposition there needs to\nbe a knowable phase relation between two superposed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121440,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "states. But that doesn’t tell us why certain\nstates that exist in the quantum world can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127040,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "survive this decoherence and become manifest\nin the classical world. To get the entire"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "answer, we need to think about quantum entanglement.\nIn fact we’ll see that the properties we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138240,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "think of as fundamental - for example the\narrangement of atoms that define a living"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142640,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or dead cat - aren’t properties of the atoms.\nRather, the information that defines these"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148560,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "classical properties exists in the fantastically\ncomplex network of entanglement between those"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154800,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "individual quantum system and their environment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159120,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The ideas I’m about to lay out are mostly\ndue to Wojciech Zurek, who calls this framework"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165520,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum darwinism - and you’ll see why.\nTo get there we need to start by talking about"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172160,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum superposition."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176320,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some quantum particles have a property called\nquantum spin. That spin has an axis that points"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182400,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in some direction, analogous to the axis of\na rotating ball. To measure that spin axis"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187840,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you have to choose what angle you want to\nmeasure it at - say, vertically or horizontally."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192640,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Measure vertically and you might see that\nthe spin axis is pointing up or down, measure"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196480,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizontally and it’ll be pointing left\nor right. Weirdly, if you measure vertically"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201600,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and see an up spin, then measure horizontally\nyou will NOT find that there’s zero spin"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207360,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in that direction, as you’d expect for a\nclassical spinning object. Instead the electron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213440,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will randomly shift ot having left or right\nspin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217040,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the value for quantum spin depends on how you\nchoose to measure it - it depends on your"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224400,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“measurement basis.” And a well defined\nspin direction in one basis can be expressed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230720,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a superposition of two spin directions\nin another basis. For example, spin-up is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236000,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equivalently a particular combination of spin-left\nand spin-right. Same with spin-down. Spin"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242720,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "becomes defined in each new basis when you\ntry to measure it in that way, and in the process"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248640,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "becomes undefined in any previous basis. This\nis an example of how seemingly-fundamental"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255600,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum states can be expressed as superpositions\nof other states. So again - why are only certain"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261840,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum states observable on large scales?\nThe answer lies in entanglement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268640,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most famous entanglement experiment is\nthe one that Einstein came up with to demonstrate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273280,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an apparent absurdity predicted by pure quantum\nmechanics. This is the so-called Einstein-Podolsky-Rosen,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280160,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or EPR paradox. A high energy photon decays\ninto an electron and a positron. These particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287040,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both have spin values of 1/2, but the original\nphoton had spin 0. So the axes of the new"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294080,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spins have to be in opposite directions in order to cancel\nout and conserve angular momentum. But besides"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301280,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "being opposite to each other they can be aligned\nin any direction. In fact each particle is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308240,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a superposition of states - measured in\nthe vertical basis, each particle starts in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312080,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a state of both up AND down, while in the\nhorizontal each is left AND right, etc. The"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317200,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only thing we know is there’s this correlation\n- whatever spin direction gets measured for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321200,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one particle, the other particle has to be\nopposite in the same basis. We say that two"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles with these sort of correlated properties\nare entangled."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332400,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The crazy thing is if I measure the spin\nof one particle - say the electron - my choice"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337280,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of measurement basis defines the spin of the\npositron. If I measure the electron in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342800,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vertical basis and get, say, spin-up, the\npositron will become defined in that basis"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348640,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and only that basis - in this case with spin-down.\nThere’s a sense that causal influence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355840,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is transferred potentially faster than light. This is what Einstein called spooky action at a distance,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363280,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and we now know that this is a very real effect,\neven though you can’t actually transfer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368160,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "useful information this way. But we’re not\nhere to talk about this crazy result directly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373680,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- in fact we already did it a long time ago.\nWe’re here to talk about how entanglement"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378640,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is connected to measurement and the collapse\nof the wavefunction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384160,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s think about exactly what’s happening\nwhen I try to measure the spin of one of those"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388480,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entangled particles. I’ll try to do that\nin the most subtle way possible. Here’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393200,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our measuring device - it’s a series of\nmagnets designed to deflect the electron based"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398800,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on its up or down spin. Spin-up electrons\nare deflected upwards, spin-down electrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405040,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are deflected down. Then both types are deflected\nback to their original straight path. By itself"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411440,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this device isn’t useful - electrons passing\nthrough will still be in a superposition of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417200,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spin states - both up and down, as though\nthey traveled both paths through the device."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422960,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we can try to measure the spin state by\nplacing a detection apparatus on one path."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429760,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This “apparatus” is as subtle as possible:\njust a single atom with a very special property."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435440,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the electron passes close to the atom,\nthe atom flips between two states. The nature"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440720,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of those two states doesn’t matter as long\nas it doesn’t change the spin or sap energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445600,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the electron. One possibility would be\nthat the spin of one of the electrons inside"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451680,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the atom gets flipped. But for simplicity,\nwe’ll call these two states off and on."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457680,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so, we put this atom along the up path\nof our device, and we start it out in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463920,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "off state. If our electron takes the up path\nit flips the atom to the on state. If it takes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471040,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the down path the atom remains in the off\nstate. We should be able to just look at the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475840,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atom to learn the electron’s path, and so\nlearn its spin. But what happens after the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482880,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electron passes through our device. Does its\nspin count as being measured? Does the positron’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489360,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spin immediately become defined? Well, actually no! After the electron passes through our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497040,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "device, we still have a superposition of states\n- but now that superposition includes the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502560,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atom. We have to think of the electron PLUS\nthe atom as being in a joint superposed state"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508800,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of: electron up, atom on AND electron down,\natom off. The atom’s state is now correlated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517440,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the electron’s state and  the two are\nentangled. Which means the atom’s state"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521920,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is also entangled with the positron’s state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524480,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We could prove that by measuring the spin\nof the positrion, which would instantaneously"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528640,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "influence the combined state of the atom and the\nelectron. Or we could measure the atom’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534880,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "state, which would influence the electron\nand positron. The measurement hasn't actually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539120,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "happened yet. The wave function hasn't collapsed.\nHere’s more evidence, even with the vertically-aligned"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546160,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "magnetic fields, which only affect the vertical\ncomponent of the electron’s spin, we haven’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551840,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even defined the basis that the electron’s\nspin as up or down. It’s still undefined."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559440,
    "dDurationMs": 9040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "See, the entangled atom now holds information\nabout the electron’s left-right spin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Whereas that information used to be expressible\nas a superposition of the electron’s up-down"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573760,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "status, now it’s hidden in a superposition\nof the atom’s on-off status. Essentially,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580960,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "phase information got transfered from the\nelectron to the atom. I won’t go into the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585120,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "details here - suffice to say that it's possible\nto recover the horizontal spin information,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590640,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even if it’s more straightforward to recover\nthe vertical spin because of how we set up"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594800,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the device."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so our atomic measurement device doesn’t\n“collapse the wavefunction.” It doesn't settle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601920,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measurement basis. So where does that happen?\nIn order for us to be aware of a measurement"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607760,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we look at a macroscopic indicator - say,\nthe pointer on a dial of our instrument."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There's a chain of quantum systems between that original atom and the pointer. Information spreads"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619600,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "along this chain as these systems become entangled\nwith each other. In fact that growing web"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625760,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of entanglement always contains all of the\ninformation about the quantum system being"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630240,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tested. The problem is, we never SEE all of\nthat information. We only see the crude, macroscopic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636720,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "characteristics of the device - like the position\nof a pointer on a dial."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641840,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So Zurek figured out why this might be the case. It turns out that as more and more particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646240,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "join our entanglement web, information about\nquantum states get spread amongst them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653440,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It would be possible to extract this information\nif you could perfectly measure all particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658000,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in your measurement device. But eventually\nthis entanglement cascade reaches the surrounding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "environment - it’s no longer bounded, and\nso the information about most quantum states"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668640,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can’t be recovered."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671680,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there are certain very special quantum\nstates whose information does NOT get hopelessly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677680,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mixed through this entanglement web. These\nare properties of the quantum system - measurement"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683280,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bases - whose information is relatively simply\nreflected throughout the surrounding environment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690240,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now we call these pointer states. Pointer states are robustly copied and spread until the states"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697840,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the surrounding macroscopic environment\n- for example our dial’s pointer - are strongly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703360,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "correlated with these states of our quantum\nsystem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what determines whether a quantum state\ncan be a pointer state? Well to some extent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713600,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the way you set up the experiment - whether\nyou align your magnetic field vertically or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718240,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizontally. But ultimately they are states\nthat can survive contact with the environment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724960,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We say that the environment selects certain\nsets of states to propogate - Zurek uses the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731120,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "term einselection, for environmentally induced\nsuperselection. And he coined the term quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737360,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "darwinism too, because we have these more\n“fit” states surviving and being replicated."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s no meaningful mutation and natural\nselection, so the link to evolution is dubious."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750320,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Still, makes for a great youtube video title."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so how does any of this explain Schrodinger’s\nCat? Well an important example of a pointer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758800,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "state is the position of a particle. Most\nquantum interactions depend heavily on the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763280,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relative location of interacting particles.\nTherefore information about relative particle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768080,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "locations is robustly shared and propagated\nthrough the entanglement network. The individual"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775760,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles do NOT have well-defined locations\n- they stay quantum-weird. But the entanglement"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782000,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "network has this sort of collective consensus\nabout those locations. The structure of macroscopic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789040,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "objects - including cats and dials - are strongly\ndefined by the relative positions of their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795280,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles. Therefore the states of cats and\ndials will always appear well defined at least to us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802160,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I should say none of this fully solves the measurement\nproblem it only tells us why certain quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807200,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "states are observable on macroscopic scales while Schroedinger Cat states - weird superpositions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814080,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are not. It still doesn't tell us why we observe\none pointer state over another unless of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "course there are multiple of these entanglement\nnetworks aka worlds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827200,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the way - in case you missed it, I just\ntold you how reality works. At least according"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831360,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the framework of decoherence and these propagating pointer states. The observable qualities of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837760,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reality - object positions, feline mortality\nstatuses, even the results of quantum measurements"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844480,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- do NOT exist in the underlying quantum objects.\nQuantum objects remain in undefined and superposed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851680,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "states with no prefered basis for observation.\nNo, the macroscopic observables only exist"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858800,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a sort of mutual agreement across the network\nof entanglements that connect those quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 865760,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "systems. In a sense, WE exist in such a network.\nIn this web of mutually consistent entanglement,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873520,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even spooky-action-at-a-distance entangled\nparticles maintain their correlations, no"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879280,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter how far separated in boring old space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883680,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to the Great Courses Plus for supporting PBS Digital Studios."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886560,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Great Courses Plus is a subscription based video on demand learning service"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with lectures and course by great professors from top universities and institutions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895360,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Through your subscription you'll get access to a library of lectures about anything that interests you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901280,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Science, Math, History, Literature, or even how to cook, play chess or become a better photographer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905600,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One exciting new course is the Theory of Everything hosted by our friend, Dr. Don Lincoln."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911120,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the course you'll confront the incompatibility between the standard model of physics and general relativity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917440,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And learn what cutting edge science is doing to reconcile these two great theories."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922000,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To learn more www.thegreatcoursesplus.com/spacetime"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925920,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or click on the link in the description to start your trial today."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928400,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Last week we talked about a thought experiment\nto test the many worlds interpretation of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 932800,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum mechanics - quantum immortality, aka\nquantum suicide, in which a physicist will"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938640,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "survive a long chain of deadly quantum events\nONLY if all possible histories - or worlds"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945680,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- eventuate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 947200,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some of you would like clarification on how\nthis experiment actually proves many worlds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950960,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After all, isn't the chance of finding yourself\nin the rare \"survival\" timeline the same between"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956080,
    "dDurationMs": 8800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many worlds and copenhagen. Well, that depends\non your definition of \"yourself\". If by that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964880,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you mean any given version of you chosen randomly\nfrom all possible future timelines - then"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970320,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "yes, chances are that version will die. The\ndifference between many worlds and copenhagen"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975840,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is that in the former there's a version of\nyou that survives - and that version actually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 981600,
    "dDurationMs": 8320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eventuates. In Copenhagen, its most likely that all of the very rare survival timelines don't happen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989920,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anonymous had an excellent insight. If every\npossible combination of quantum events exists"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 996960,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and if the increase of entropy is probabilistic, then is it inevitable that there are exceptionally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1003920,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rare timelines where the universe gets to\na state of low entropy to produce another"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1010640,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Big Bang? In other words - if all possible\nfutures occur in Many Worlds, do some of those"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1016400,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lead to new Big Bangs. Well, the key phrase here is \"possible future\". Do the laws of physics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1022720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "allow an entropy fluctuation large enough\nto produce a new big bang? Could a random"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028800,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perfect alignment of all particle trajectories\nproduce a big bang density? Maybe - some physicsts"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1035440,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "think this may be how our big bang happened.\nIf so, then the never-ending global wavefunction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043520,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the Many worlds interpretation could indeed\nbe a big-bang machine. On the other hand,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1049760,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if the evolution of that wavefunction can't\nconceivable produce big bang conditions,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1054240,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then no - only *possible* futures happen,\nas Seraphina explained in that same comment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1060560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ultimate Hair Dryer asks how the many world\ninterpretation handles the probabilistic interpretation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1066320,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the wave function. And then goes on to\ncorrectly answer their own question - to paraphrase:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1073280,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whereas the Copenhagen interpretation states\nthat only one branch of the wavefunction survives"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1079440,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measurement, corresponding to one possible\nresult of that measurement. And the wavefunction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1084160,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "itself defines the probability of a given\nbranch \"collapsing\". The Many Worlds interpretation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1090160,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "states that all branches survive, and the\nmeasurement result that we observe depends"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1095920,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on which branch we find ourselves in. However\nnot all measurement results are equal. You"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1102400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can imagine the wavefunction as consisting\nof very many fine trajectories - possible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1107360,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "histories by which the system can evolve.\nSome final results are more probable because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1112400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more histories lead to that result. Most of\nthe time you will naturally find yourself"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1117520,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in one of the more common histories - corresponding\nto more probable measurement results. It's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1124240,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "analogous to statistical mechanics, in which\nmany different arrangements of air in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1130400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "room lead to the same measurement of temperature\nand pressure - so we'll almost always observe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1135520,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those macroscopic measureables."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1138160,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thatisjustgreat declines our offer of quantum\nimmortality, saying \"I'll stick with classical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1143200,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "immortality, thank you\". thanks for commenting\nKeith Richards, I hope you enjoy watching"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1148160,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the heat death of the universe."
    } ]
  } ]
}
